  "drawer_model_notes": {
    "framework": "Claude Code SDK subagents dispatched via Anthropic Workflow scripts (tools/dispatcher.py + workflow.js/*.js under scratchpad/)",
    "underlying_model": "Claude Opus (family) — the specific model ID at drawer invocation is whatever the SDK/harness resolves at the time of the batch. See per-batch judged_at timestamps below to correlate with API model-version notes if needed.",
    "model_change_disclosure": "The experiment ran across ~1 month (2026-07-12 through 2026-08-13, judged_at range). Model updates during this window may have shifted underlying capability. This is flagged as a limitation in REPORT_DRAFT.md §4.5 and elaborated below in `possible_model_drift_windows`. G1-G4 main-experiment batches ran continuously 2026-07 to 2026-08-08; G5 catch-up ran 2026-08-08 through 2026-08-13. Every G5 attempt was drawn AFTER the last G1-G4 attempt (bootstrap), so any late-window model improvement is systematically attributed to G5 in the raw data.",
    "sampling_settings": "Default Claude Code SDK subagent settings (no temperature override). All drawer subagents ran with the standard 'general-purpose' subagent type, max ~10 tool calls per attempt, ONE within-item revision allowed.",
    "judge": "Single human (first author), blind to group identity per attempt via tools/judge_blind.py randomization",
    "harness_version": "Claude Code CLI (Opus-family model) + Anthropic Workflow tool. Repository state at commit 6b761109 (feature/rapidocr-judge branch)."
  },
  "possible_model_drift_windows": {
    "main_experiment_window": "2026-07-12 (v3 scaffold) → 2026-08-03 (post-v14 rollback + B12v2 re-run for G4)",
    "g5_catch_up_window": "2026-08-08 → 2026-08-13",
    "drift_risk_note": "G5 was run entirely AFTER G1-G4's main-experiment window. Any capability improvement in the underlying model between the two windows would inflate G5's numbers relative to G1-G4. The paper's cross-group comparisons should be read with this in mind. However: (a) G3 (same code format as G5, no MMH) run in the earlier window achieved 36% cumulative success; G5 (same code format, with MMH) run later achieved 54% — the +18pt gap is much larger than plausible drift and dominantly attributable to MMH. (b) The Format effect comparison (G4 vs G5) is more drift-vulnerable since G4 was in the earlier window and G5 later; a hypothetical model-drift-alone hypothesis would predict G5 > G4 uniformly, which it doesn't — G5 leads on PASS but G4 leads on A rate. The pattern is inconsistent with pure drift."
  },
